# Finiteness and degree bound descend under coefficientwise field maps

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.finiteDimensional_and_finrank_adjoin_le_of_eq_coeffMap`
(`79b963aa-eada-592a-846b-a690dd73f1d7`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `dc78e285-f6a2-511c-b921-94f50248da88`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K_0$ and $K$ be fields, $\varphi\colon K_0\to K$ a ring homomorphism, and $\Gamma$ a subgroup
of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$. For a field $L$ write $F_L$ for [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC L
Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101), the intermediate field of $L((q))$ generated over $L$ by the set
of quotients $\iota_L(p_f)/\iota_L(p_g)$, where $f,g$ are modular forms of one and the same weight
$k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, $p_f,p_g$ are integral power series
realising their $q$-expansions (the project's `IsIntegralQExp`), $\iota_L$ denotes coefficientwise
reduction of an integral series into $L((q))$, and the denominator $\iota_L(p_g)$ is nonzero. Let
$x_0\in F_{K_0}$ and $x\in F_K$ be such that $x_0$ is transcendental over $K_0$ as an element of
$K_0((q))$, and such that $x$ equals the image of $x_0$ under [`ModularCurve.coeffMap
φ`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16), the coefficientwise application of $\varphi$ from
$K_0((q))$ to $K((q))$. Assume $F_K$ is finite-dimensional over the simple extension $K(x)$ inside
$F_K$. Then $F_{K_0}$ is finite-dimensional over $K_0(x_0)$, and $[F_{K_0}:K_0(x_0)]\le[F_K:K(x)]$.

This is the elementary half of the comparison of degrees of function fields under extension of the
field of constants, realised concretely for subfields of formal Laurent series fields, where it
rests on the linear disjointness of the constant field from Laurent series with coefficients in the
image of $\varphi$. It is used in the project to transfer degree bounds for $q$-expansion function
fields over a base field to a larger coefficient field, and feeds the estimates relating the index
of $\Gamma_H$ to the degree of the extension generated by the modular function $j$.

